PUBLIC RESPONSE: {E. Yes, because James reasonably believed that there was an imminent threat of death or serious bodily harm, and disclosure was necessary to prevent the possible harm.

This scenario is governed by the Law Society of Ontario’s Rules of Professional Conduct (Rule 3.3-3), which expressly permits (but does not require) disclosure of confidential client information when a lawyer reasonably believes there is an imminent risk of death or serious bodily harm to an identifiable person or group and disclosure is necessary to prevent that harm. Eric’s explicit statement that he had already purchased a gun and intended to kill both himself and his ex-wife “tonight” meets the imminence threshold. James’s disclosure of contact and location information was narrowly tailored to enable police to locate Eric and intervene, satisfying the necessity element. Choices A–D are inconsistent with the rule: the threat was same-day, confidentiality is not absolute, the information shared was appropriate to the purpose, and police requests alone do not create a duty to disclose.}

}

